  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,\u2016x\u2016=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $9$ and $6,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis.",
  "worked_solution": "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,\u2016x\u2016=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,\u2016x\u2016=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016Ax{\u2016}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\u2016x\u2016=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $9,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{\u2016x\u2016=1}{\u2016Ax{\u2016}^{2}}=9\\,\u27f9\\,{max}_{\u2016x\u2016=1}{\u2016Ax\u2016}=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $3.$",
